Yes. There are several different kinds of “power” braided together there, and separating them actually makes the idea stronger.

The Sun and Moon have direct physical effects: illumination, heating, seasons, tides, circadian entrainment, navigation cues, predator–prey behavior, spawning or reproductive timing in some species, and so on. Those effects then create reliable patterns. Once organisms learn or evolve around those patterns, the celestial cycle acquires another layer of causal force: not because the object is “telling” the organism what to do, but because the organism has incorporated the pattern into its control system.

Then humans add a third layer: symbolic causation. We notice recurrence, personify it, name it, build calendars around it, write myths around it, organize rituals around it, navigate by it, and eventually make decisions because of the story attached to it. At that point the story itself begins producing consequences in the world.

That is important because “not physically caused by X” does not mean “X has no causal consequences.” If enough people coordinate around Orion, a solstice, a lunar month, a flag, a god, a market narrative, or a number, the representation starts affecting behavior. The causal chain can be:

celestial pattern → perception → interpretation → shared narrative → coordinated behavior → material consequence

Stars and constellations absolutely can participate in that. Their direct physical influence on an individual human is tiny compared with the Sun or Moon, but their informational and cultural leverage can be enormous. Polaris changes navigation because of where it appears in the sky. Constellations helped societies preserve seasonal calendars and stories. Astrology can affect behavior when people believe and act on it even without demonstrating the claimed astronomical mechanism. The symbol becomes an operator through the humans carrying it.

And your last point is especially important: explanations are interventions.

When an ambiguous event occurs, there may be dozens of compatible narratives. Once one is selected, people start allocating attention, predicting, preparing, avoiding, seeking, remembering, and coordinating according to it. That changes subsequent observations. You get a feedback loop:

random/ambiguous event → explanation → behavior → altered environment → new evidence → strengthened or weakened explanation

So the explanation is no longer merely describing reality. It has entered the dynamics of the system.

That creates both leverage and danger. You can deliberately choose interpretations that generate curiosity, resilience, coordination, experimentation, or meaning. But if you choose an explanation that creates fear, false certainty, scapegoating, or reckless confidence, that story can manufacture the very evidence later used to justify it.

The stronger move is therefore not “believe whatever explanation is useful.” It is:

hold multiple causal models → know which parts are physical, biological, psychological, social, and symbolic → observe what each model causes people to do → deliberately cultivate the models whose downstream effects you actually want.

That turns myth from a claim that has to defeat physics into something much more interesting: a pattern humans can instantiate, inhabit, and use to organize reality.